# One Output Is A Chip, Many Outputs Are Cards

How many outputs a turn produced decides how they are drawn. This is not a
per-surface taste call and it is not something a caller opts into — it is the
count, and nothing else.

- **Exactly one output** → the **landscape chip**. A full-width card in the
  reading column: 52px preview on the left, the output name beside it at 14px,
  the gold Gilded Grain stroke, the `vN` badge on the top-right corner. It
  reads as a line of the answer, because that is what it is.
- **More than one output** → the **portrait cards**, side by side on the
  carousel rail. 120px wide, a 108px preview on top, the name small and
  centered underneath. The rail bleeds to the chat module's edges and scrolls,
  and a lead line above it says how many landed: *"Here are **10 outputs** for
  you to review."*

A single output is never a portrait card, and a run of outputs is never a
stack of landscape rows. Those were the two bugs this rule exists to stop: one
chart alone in a 120px card with a name too small to read, and ten landscape
rows laddering down the thread.

## The count is the turn's, not the slot's

Read the count across everything the **turn** surfaced, before the first one
is drawn. Three charts and a report is four outputs — cards. One report on its
own is one output — a chip, even when the answer above it is long.

**Versions are not a count.** Redoing the same output stacks versions into one
slot; that slot is still one output. A single output redone three times is a
landscape chip holding a cascade of three landscape cards, never a portrait
strip. The portrait card exists inside a multi-output rail, and a card there
can itself carry one version or a fanned cascade of several.

## How it is wired

`surfaceSet(items, { rail })` in `pages/wiseai.html` is the door. A turn hands
it the whole set and it applies the rule: one item goes straight to
`surfaceBlock` untouched, several get the rail, `cardLayout: 'portrait'` and
the count line on the first. A turn that builds its own run — the atlas, the
brand overview — does the same thing inline against a count it derives
(`atlasOutputCount()`), never a literal.

Below that, `surfaceCardHtml()` draws the slot (`cardLayout: 'portrait'` picks
the card, its absence picks the chip) and `postSurfaceBlock()` puts the cards
on a shared `.sc-surface-rail.sc-surface-rail--bleed`, writing `railLead`
above the first. The shared classes are `.sc-surface-card`,
`.sc-surface-card--portrait`, `.sc-surface-rail` and `.sc-surface-rail-lead`
in `pages/wise.css`.

Outputs still arrive after the answer has finished (see the turn-stage-order
rule) and the pane stays shut until the member taps a chip or a card (see the
outputs-open-on-request rule). This rule only decides what the member is
looking at.

## Rules for the agent

- Never hand-pick a layout. A turn that can surface more than one output hands
  the whole set to `surfaceSet` and lets the count choose. Calling
  `surfaceBlock` two or three times in a row is the bug this rule names.
- Never write the count as a literal. Derive it from the set, so adding an
  output cannot leave the lead saying the old number.
- Do not fork the two looks per page or invent a third size — see the
  nothing-page-local rule. A new host renders the same two classes.
- The catalog states this rule on the **Output chips** card in
  `pages/all-modules.html`. If you change the behavior, change the card.

## Verify

`scripts/_output_count_shape_probe.py` drives a one-output turn and a
multi-output turn and checks the shape each one landed in. Run it in both
themes after touching any of this.
